From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: iris: send the raw and visible frame sizes to the gen1
 encoder

The gen1 encoder set its input frame size from fmt_src, whose width and
height are the buffer size aligned to 128x32, and its output frame size
from fmt_dst, which holds that same aligned size after an OUTPUT S_FMT,
or the requested size aligned to the codec block size after a CAPTURE
S_FMT or a crop. For a width that is not a multiple of 128 (a 1080x1920
portrait camera stream) the firmware therefore read a 1152-wide picture,
stride padding included, and scaled it into a 1088-wide output with no
SPS cropping: the right edge of every frame showed the zeroed padding as
a green band and the picture was squeezed.

Send the raw frame size for the input, as the gen2 path does with
enc_raw_width/height, and the requested output size kept in
enc_scale_width/height for the output. The stride and scanlines still
reach the firmware separately in UNCOMPRESSED_PLANE_ACTUAL_INFO. The
firmware pads the coded picture to whole macroblocks and signals the
cropping in the SPS.

Frame sizes that are already aligned (1920x1088) are sent unchanged.
gen1 has no input crop offsets, so a crop smaller than the raw frame is
still scaled rather than cropped, as before.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 .../qcom/iris/iris_hfi_gen1_command.c         | 35 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_gen1_command.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_gen1_command.c
index af8106071..b43d86c57 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_gen1_command.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_gen1_command.c
@@ -842,20 +842,41 @@ static int iris_hfi_gen1_set_resolution(struct iris_inst *inst, u32 plane)
 
 	if (!iris_drc_pending(inst) && !(inst->sub_state & IRIS_INST_SUB_FIRST_IPSC)) {
 		fs.buffer_type = HFI_BUFFER_INPUT;
-		fs.width = inst->fmt_src->fmt.pix_mp.width;
-		fs.height = inst->fmt_src->fmt.pix_mp.height;
+		if (inst->domain == ENCODER) {
+			/*
+			 * The raw frame size, not the 128x32-aligned buffer size in
+			 * fmt_src. The stride and scanlines are sent separately in
+			 * UNCOMPRESSED_PLANE_ACTUAL_INFO; with the aligned size here
+			 * the firmware treats the stride padding as picture and
+			 * scales it into the output.
+			 */
+			fs.width = inst->enc_raw_width;
+			fs.height = inst->enc_raw_height;
+		} else {
+			fs.width = inst->fmt_src->fmt.pix_mp.width;
+			fs.height = inst->fmt_src->fmt.pix_mp.height;
+		}
 
 		ret = hfi_gen1_set_property(inst, ptype, &fs, sizeof(fs));
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
 	}
-	if (inst->domain == DECODER)
+	if (inst->domain == DECODER) {
 		fs.buffer_type = HFI_BUFFER_OUTPUT2;
-	else
+		fs.width = inst->fmt_dst->fmt.pix_mp.width;
+		fs.height = inst->fmt_dst->fmt.pix_mp.height;
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * The requested output size kept in enc_scale (set by a CAPTURE
+		 * S_FMT or a crop, the raw size by default), not the aligned size
+		 * in fmt_dst: the firmware pads to whole macroblocks itself and
+		 * signals the cropping in the SPS. gen1 has no input crop
+		 * offsets, so a crop smaller than the raw frame is scaled.
+		 */
 		fs.buffer_type = HFI_BUFFER_OUTPUT;
-
-	fs.width = inst->fmt_dst->fmt.pix_mp.width;
-	fs.height = inst->fmt_dst->fmt.pix_mp.height;
+		fs.width = inst->enc_scale_width;
+		fs.height = inst->enc_scale_height;
+	}
 
 	return hfi_gen1_set_property(inst, ptype, &fs, sizeof(fs));
 }
